WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.544 --> 00:00:02.576
- The actual making of a film,

00:00:02.576 --> 00:00:04.889
the day to day going to work,
the construction of a film,

00:00:04.889 --> 00:00:06.013
has always been my favorite thing.

00:00:06.013 --> 00:00:07.011
I'm going to go stand in the mirror,

00:00:07.011 --> 00:00:08.870
and my wife's over there
watching, reading something,

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:09.762
I'm going to stand in the mirror

00:00:09.762 --> 00:00:10.679
and I'm going to practice a move.

00:00:10.679 --> 00:00:11.512
This is really embarrassing.

00:00:11.512 --> 00:00:12.345
Well, too bad, man.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.402
Do it in the mirror right now.

00:00:13.402 --> 00:00:16.569
The $320, I was like, "Is this legal?"

00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:19.731
'Cause it was so much fun and I was,

00:00:19.731 --> 00:00:21.740
(laughs)

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.952
I was really looking
over my shoulder going.

00:00:23.952 --> 00:00:24.785
(laughs)

00:00:24.785 --> 00:00:27.538
Being an actor, or even being
an artist, and I had studied

00:00:27.538 --> 00:00:30.737
in school behind the camera,
was not even in the vernacular

00:00:30.737 --> 00:00:32.113
of my dreams.

00:00:32.113 --> 00:00:34.657
- He's an Academy Award
winning American actor.

00:00:34.657 --> 00:00:37.226
He achieved critical success
for portraying a cowboy

00:00:37.226 --> 00:00:40.708
who was diagnosed with AIDS in
the Dallas Buyers Club movie.

00:00:40.708 --> 00:00:44.261
He also starred in HBO's first
season of True Detective,

00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:46.193
for which he earned an Emmy nomination.

00:00:46.193 --> 00:00:48.494
He's Matthew McConaughey
and here's my take on his

00:00:48.494 --> 00:00:50.128
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:50.128 --> 00:00:52.183
Number one is my personal
favorite, and make sure to

00:00:52.183 --> 00:00:53.739
stick around all the way to end for some

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:55.285
special bonus clips.

00:00:55.285 --> 00:00:57.305
Also, as you're watching and
listening, if Matthew says

00:00:57.305 --> 00:00:59.394
something that you really
enjoy, make sure to leave

00:00:59.394 --> 00:01:01.706
it in the comments below
with quotation marks,

00:01:01.706 --> 00:01:03.967
so we know what you really enjoy.

00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:05.045
(air rushes)

00:01:05.045 --> 00:01:07.135
(thuds)

00:01:07.135 --> 00:01:09.564
- The making, the actual making
of a film, the day to day

00:01:09.564 --> 00:01:11.288
going to work, the
construction of the film

00:01:11.288 --> 00:01:12.904
has always been my favorite thing.

00:01:12.904 --> 00:01:15.714
I like making a film much more
than I like watching them.

00:01:15.714 --> 00:01:18.033
I do. Even the one's I'm in.

00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:21.465
I really love the coming
together with a whole bunch of

00:01:21.465 --> 00:01:24.104
people having an idea on
paper, and everyone's an

00:01:24.104 --> 00:01:27.396
expert at what they do and
you're off on this turn

00:01:27.396 --> 00:01:30.064
and you got ideas but
then you just let them go

00:01:30.064 --> 00:01:31.693
and see what it can become.

00:01:31.693 --> 00:01:33.879
I love the making,
that's when I'm happiest,

00:01:33.879 --> 00:01:37.768
is when I'm making a
film or telling a story,

00:01:37.768 --> 00:01:39.646
the day to day.

00:01:39.646 --> 00:01:42.896
And more so than watching them, really.

00:01:45.308 --> 00:01:47.045
Look, I kind of defined that for myself

00:01:47.045 --> 00:01:49.242
about four years ago.

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:52.597
Process. Stay in process.
Head down, in process.

00:01:52.597 --> 00:01:55.847
Stay in your bubble and when it's over,

00:01:57.294 --> 00:01:59.005
be that fulfillment.

00:01:59.005 --> 00:02:00.974
Notice, when I started noticing
that fulfillment of it,

00:02:00.974 --> 00:02:04.353
I just finished, well
that's enough, that's it.

00:02:04.353 --> 00:02:08.529
I don't know if it's even
coming out, I don't even know,

00:02:08.529 --> 00:02:09.845
I don't know what it's going to turn into.

00:02:09.845 --> 00:02:11.739
Man I hope we got it.

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:14.188
And, I hope the director
put something good together,

00:02:14.188 --> 00:02:15.021
but I don't know.

00:02:15.021 --> 00:02:15.963
Nothing I can do about that.

00:02:15.963 --> 00:02:18.180
All I know is that this
experience, that I said,

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.176
go have the experience, McConaughey.

00:02:20.176 --> 00:02:22.662
And, if I can love this
experience and at the end of it,

00:02:22.662 --> 00:02:25.330
I'm like may never even
hear that film again.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.615
May never even know that
story again, it may be gone.

00:02:27.615 --> 00:02:29.471
Let me find out that all
the negatives got fried

00:02:29.471 --> 00:02:31.590
in the x-ray machine, there is
no film, but I've got to be,

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:32.423
(laughs)

00:02:32.423 --> 00:02:35.367
that's okay, I had an experience.

00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:38.534
When I got selfish for that, for that,

00:02:40.672 --> 00:02:44.982
the experience, that process,
enjoying that process,

00:02:44.982 --> 00:02:48.143
the day to day architecture
of making a movie

00:02:48.143 --> 00:02:49.305
and creating a role.

00:02:49.305 --> 00:02:52.222
That's when I've been the happiest.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:58.085
And more results have come
that way, in an odd way.

00:02:58.085 --> 00:02:59.585
- Yes.
- You know?

00:03:01.019 --> 00:03:03.355
But, when I started to
really approaching them

00:03:03.355 --> 00:03:05.513
as this is a finite thing and
this is just an experience,

00:03:05.513 --> 00:03:08.258
other than that I'm not
producing this thing.

00:03:08.258 --> 00:03:09.753
I'm not the one editing, I don't know what

00:03:09.753 --> 00:03:12.392
it's going to be, that's it.

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:14.003
And then, all of a sudden
they come out and you go,

00:03:14.003 --> 00:03:15.648
oh, I think we got a good one.

00:03:15.648 --> 00:03:18.416
- [Interviewer] So, are you
surprised by the places you went

00:03:18.416 --> 00:03:19.251
in that performance?

00:03:19.251 --> 00:03:20.987
Are you surprised when you see it back

00:03:20.987 --> 00:03:21.820
or are you already--

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.010
- No, not after I engaged,
not after I took the dare.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.393
Soon as I got off the phone
call with him and then also when

00:03:29.393 --> 00:03:32.569
he said it's not in the picture now, but I

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:35.045
think it'd be great if
Dallas danced at the end.

00:03:35.045 --> 00:03:36.880
And my heartbeat went up
on the phone and I started

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.963
breaking a cold sweat like,
like going no that's not.

00:03:39.963 --> 00:03:41.824
And, as soon as I went oh no that's not,

00:03:41.824 --> 00:03:45.991
the other devil in my ear
said I (bleep) dare you do,

00:03:47.273 --> 00:03:49.295
and I just went of course.

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:53.466
Yes, because I immediately
noticed, if you don't

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:55.741
you're wussing out dude,
are you kidding me?

00:03:55.741 --> 00:03:57.657
You will forever regret, you have to.

00:03:57.657 --> 00:03:59.851
So, as soon as I got over
that, I was like well

00:03:59.851 --> 00:04:01.963
that's inevitable, I'm doing it.

00:04:01.963 --> 00:04:06.367
Now, I got to bust my ass
to go do it the best I can.

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:08.498
And, there was a lot of fear in that.

00:04:08.498 --> 00:04:09.831
I mean, that was

00:04:12.598 --> 00:04:15.451
some majorly good anxiety and fear.

00:04:15.451 --> 00:04:18.683
And then, now I'm
saying, and by that time,

00:04:18.683 --> 00:04:22.711
now everything, my choices
from that phone call onward

00:04:22.711 --> 00:04:25.777
anything I was ever set
you have to go (bleep).

00:04:25.777 --> 00:04:27.480
Nah dude, go, all the way.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.004
Go, meet midnight, yes.

00:04:29.004 --> 00:04:31.438
Can you all meet in front
of the choreograph session?

00:04:31.438 --> 00:04:34.452
Eight a.m. Sunday morning,
do you got an hour?

00:04:34.452 --> 00:04:36.977
I'm going to go stand in the
mirror and my wife's over there

00:04:36.977 --> 00:04:38.073
reading something, I'm going
to stand in the mirror,

00:04:38.073 --> 00:04:38.906
I'm going to practice a move.

00:04:38.906 --> 00:04:39.742
This is really embarrassing.

00:04:39.742 --> 00:04:41.792
Well, too bad man, do it
in the mirror right now

00:04:41.792 --> 00:04:44.196
because the less embarrassing that gets

00:04:44.196 --> 00:04:45.511
hopefully the more comfortable you'll be

00:04:45.511 --> 00:04:47.463
when you're out there
in front of 80 people

00:04:47.463 --> 00:04:49.058
and a bunch of cameras.

00:04:49.058 --> 00:04:52.725
And, one thing you do
when you go out there,

00:04:53.623 --> 00:04:54.756
don't come up short.

00:04:54.756 --> 00:04:56.063
(laughs)

00:04:56.063 --> 00:04:57.396
Go, all the way.

00:04:59.384 --> 00:05:02.048
- So, even with these
movies under your belt

00:05:02.048 --> 00:05:04.183
you came out to L.A. and
you were still thinking

00:05:04.183 --> 00:05:05.797
it was going to be for P.A. work.

00:05:05.797 --> 00:05:09.287
- Still thought it was
going to be P.A. work.

00:05:09.287 --> 00:05:12.082
I mean, I didn't know, it was still,

00:05:12.082 --> 00:05:14.721
I was the leading (mumbles),
I mean, I sure did like

00:05:14.721 --> 00:05:16.895
that I was doing it and I
had people and I couldn't,

00:05:16.895 --> 00:05:19.478
honestly at that time the $320.

00:05:21.559 --> 00:05:23.425
I was like is this legal?

00:05:23.425 --> 00:05:24.646
'Cause it was so much fun.

00:05:24.646 --> 00:05:25.611
And then, I was

00:05:25.611 --> 00:05:27.568
(laughs)

00:05:27.568 --> 00:05:29.256
I was really looking
over my shoulder going.

00:05:29.256 --> 00:05:31.888
(laughs)

00:05:31.888 --> 00:05:33.719
And, I kept getting invited back.

00:05:33.719 --> 00:05:35.931
And then, I had people coming
up and going, "Hey man,

00:05:35.931 --> 00:05:36.764
"You're good at this."

00:05:36.764 --> 00:05:39.866
And, I was like, "Well thanks,
'cause it sure is fun."

00:05:39.866 --> 00:05:41.949
So, I really didn't know.

00:05:43.584 --> 00:05:45.698
I guess secretly, if I look back at it

00:05:45.698 --> 00:05:47.908
and I look at my diaries
there was hope underlying,

00:05:47.908 --> 00:05:50.884
but I always feel like I
need to go out and get a job.

00:05:50.884 --> 00:05:53.851
And, if I can pursue acting on the side,

00:05:53.851 --> 00:05:56.500
get a P.A. job, get in
the storytelling business

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.167
behind the camera and let's see.

00:06:01.654 --> 00:06:04.539
Then, another funny thing happened.

00:06:04.539 --> 00:06:07.074
That first audition when
I got out here was for

00:06:07.074 --> 00:06:09.911
a casting director named Hank McCann.

00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:11.018
You know who I'm talking about?

00:06:11.018 --> 00:06:13.855
It was for a film called Boys on the Side.

00:06:13.855 --> 00:06:16.176
And, I went and read and
he goes, "I think you did

00:06:16.176 --> 00:06:20.823
"really good, I'll have you for
a call back sometime later."

00:06:20.823 --> 00:06:23.990
I went off from there, I get called in

00:06:25.533 --> 00:06:29.594
for a baseball film,
Angels in the Outfield.

00:06:29.594 --> 00:06:33.091
I walk in the door,
there's a bunch of funny

00:06:33.091 --> 00:06:34.951
peculiarities on how the hell I got to be

00:06:34.951 --> 00:06:35.892
standing here today.

00:06:35.892 --> 00:06:37.151
(laughs)

00:06:37.151 --> 00:06:38.870
I walk in the door, I was back lit.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:42.440
I had an American cap on, it
was a Warner Brothers lot.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:43.820
I open up the door.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.618
It was back lit in the
afternoon, producer's sitting

00:06:45.618 --> 00:06:47.031
on the couch he looks up and goes,

00:06:47.031 --> 00:06:50.648
"Hey, that's (mumbles),
the all American kid."

00:06:50.648 --> 00:06:51.786
(laughs)

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:53.111
And then like, he goes,
"You ever play baseball?"

00:06:53.111 --> 00:06:53.944
I said, "12 years."

00:06:53.944 --> 00:06:54.904
He goes, "you got the job."

00:06:54.904 --> 00:06:56.987
(laughs)

00:06:59.808 --> 00:07:02.414
So, they send me, they pay me schedule f.

00:07:02.414 --> 00:07:04.776
48 five,
(laughs)

00:07:04.776 --> 00:07:07.712
$48,500 to go play baseball
for 11 weeks in Oakland.

00:07:07.712 --> 00:07:09.988
Again, I'm going, "What
is this business, man?"

00:07:09.988 --> 00:07:11.095
(laughs)

00:07:11.095 --> 00:07:12.035
But, it happened.

00:07:12.035 --> 00:07:13.617
And, during that I got
called back to read for

00:07:13.617 --> 00:07:15.486
Harper Ross and Boys on
the Side and got that role.

00:07:15.486 --> 00:07:18.740
So, that Cohen Brothers
film that got pushed,

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:21.784
that P.A. job that I would
have taken if it was there

00:07:21.784 --> 00:07:25.606
never happened and I haven't P.A.'d since.

00:07:25.606 --> 00:07:26.627
(laughs)

00:07:26.627 --> 00:07:29.329
(crowd applauds)

00:07:29.329 --> 00:07:31.191
And, I had about a year
and a half where I would

00:07:31.191 --> 00:07:32.913
audition and I would get one call back,

00:07:32.913 --> 00:07:37.093
two call backs, three call
backs, and I never got the part.

00:07:37.093 --> 00:07:38.667
And, I remember feeling
like I'm too tight,

00:07:38.667 --> 00:07:39.500
you know what I mean?

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.033
I think maybe I'm studying too much.

00:07:42.033 --> 00:07:44.092
I remember talking to myself
and, "Maybe I'm studying

00:07:44.092 --> 00:07:47.926
"too much about the import
of the line and everything

00:07:47.926 --> 00:07:49.351
"and you know what?

00:07:49.351 --> 00:07:51.519
"I think I need to go
back to doing what I did

00:07:51.519 --> 00:07:54.078
"when I first started,
which is I was just a guy

00:07:54.078 --> 00:07:55.336
"and I improvised."

00:07:55.336 --> 00:07:59.442
So, I had a film that I was
cast for and I was playing

00:07:59.442 --> 00:08:02.389
this on the border Mexican
drug lord and I told

00:08:02.389 --> 00:08:04.189
myself I'm not going to look at the sides.

00:08:04.189 --> 00:08:05.483
I'm not even going to read the script.

00:08:05.483 --> 00:08:07.086
It's an on the border Mexican drug lord,

00:08:07.086 --> 00:08:08.797
I hear the synopsis.

00:08:08.797 --> 00:08:12.865
If I know my man then
I'll show up and just,

00:08:12.865 --> 00:08:15.349
obviously, I'll read
it right before I go on

00:08:15.349 --> 00:08:16.572
and if I know my man that'll be like,

00:08:16.572 --> 00:08:17.961
"Well, that's exactly what he'd say."

00:08:17.961 --> 00:08:19.917
(laughs)

00:08:19.917 --> 00:08:22.750
Foolish, I go to set that morning,

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:26.618
all right, I'm just going
to, all I have to do

00:08:26.618 --> 00:08:28.395
is have a glance at it and it'll be like,

00:08:28.395 --> 00:08:30.113
"Yep, I know how to do it."

00:08:30.113 --> 00:08:31.671
Well, I look at it,
right, before we're about

00:08:31.671 --> 00:08:34.014
to say action and it was a page and a half

00:08:34.014 --> 00:08:36.341
monologue in Spanish.

00:08:36.341 --> 00:08:38.635
(laughs)

00:08:38.635 --> 00:08:40.382
Oh, and I knew when I
felt this strip of sweat

00:08:40.382 --> 00:08:42.031
come up on the back of my head and go down

00:08:42.031 --> 00:08:45.743
and for whatever reason
I looked over and I said,

00:08:45.743 --> 00:08:49.326
"Can you give me like 12 minutes?"

00:08:49.326 --> 00:08:50.680
Why I said 12 minutes I don't know.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.276
I figured it was like not long
enough to be inconsiderate,

00:08:53.276 --> 00:08:56.101
but long enough to maybe get
a little bit of that stuff.

00:08:56.101 --> 00:08:58.641
Anyway, I think I was horrible in it.

00:08:58.641 --> 00:09:00.385
I stammered through some Spanish

00:09:00.385 --> 00:09:02.798
and tried to look aligned.

00:09:02.798 --> 00:09:06.419
So, then I was like, "Okay,
that's not the way to go."

00:09:06.419 --> 00:09:08.316
(laughs)

00:09:08.316 --> 00:09:10.902
There's a blend here of having
to do your due diligence,

00:09:10.902 --> 00:09:13.127
do your studying but then
being able to go down

00:09:13.127 --> 00:09:15.133
the day and relax and throw it all away.

00:09:15.133 --> 00:09:19.572
I'm probably much more
intentional than people

00:09:19.572 --> 00:09:20.989
would think I am.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:23.890
I make choices.

00:09:27.654 --> 00:09:30.237
I invest in 'em. I work on 'em.

00:09:31.355 --> 00:09:34.302
And, I don't choose to
really share a lot of that,

00:09:34.302 --> 00:09:36.385
it's my own private time.

00:09:38.245 --> 00:09:40.864
I try and do the work
whether it's personally,

00:09:40.864 --> 00:09:42.301
whether it's on a role, whatever it is

00:09:42.301 --> 00:09:46.995
so I can then appear like
I'm blowing in the wind.

00:09:46.995 --> 00:09:49.364
I call it create your
own weather so you can

00:09:49.364 --> 00:09:50.222
blow in the wind.

00:09:50.222 --> 00:09:52.724
It's conservative early, liberal late

00:09:52.724 --> 00:09:53.557
is what I'm talking about.

00:09:53.557 --> 00:09:55.024
See what sandbox you're playing in.

00:09:55.024 --> 00:09:56.659
Any glass in here, no no glass.

00:09:56.659 --> 00:09:59.331
Okay, there's the
boundaries, that's the rules.

00:09:59.331 --> 00:10:03.164
Okay, now get four
dimensional in the sandbox,

00:10:04.175 --> 00:10:06.634
now blow in the breeze.

00:10:06.634 --> 00:10:08.637
I choose my direction, we're going east,

00:10:08.637 --> 00:10:10.846
we're going north, we're
going south, or we going west?

00:10:10.846 --> 00:10:13.023
All right, I got it,
this kid is going east.

00:10:13.023 --> 00:10:16.614
Well now, you got 16 lanes to swerve in,

00:10:16.614 --> 00:10:17.739
take an exit.

00:10:17.739 --> 00:10:20.354
All you got to do to get
there, don't know how

00:10:20.354 --> 00:10:21.187
we're going to get there.

00:10:21.187 --> 00:10:23.278
We may have a long put
stop at the gas station.

00:10:23.278 --> 00:10:25.525
We may pull off somewhere, we may stop.

00:10:25.525 --> 00:10:27.746
But, one thing we're not
going to do is pull a u-turn

00:10:27.746 --> 00:10:29.207
and go the other way.

00:10:29.207 --> 00:10:32.739
Just stay in direction
and swerve all you want.

00:10:32.739 --> 00:10:35.891
That's the liberal lane,
that's the blow in the wind.

00:10:35.891 --> 00:10:39.558
So, I do my pre-production
work whether it's

00:10:40.616 --> 00:10:42.986
yearly, whether it's in
the morning, when I wake up

00:10:42.986 --> 00:10:44.400
in the morning having tea with myself.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.222
I make a plan out for the
day or the week, year,

00:10:49.222 --> 00:10:51.847
and then I kind of set
it out there and say,

00:10:51.847 --> 00:10:52.786
"Now, how are we going to get there?

00:10:52.786 --> 00:10:54.199
"I'm not sure, but it's there."

00:10:54.199 --> 00:10:57.969
And, I'm happy to say that so many of my

00:10:57.969 --> 00:11:00.469
goals and dreams that I've had

00:11:02.787 --> 00:11:05.954
19 out of 20 that I wrote down in 1988

00:11:06.863 --> 00:11:10.351
I completely forgot about 'em,
completely forgot about 'em.

00:11:10.351 --> 00:11:14.019
I pulled the diary out last
year and 19 out of 20 I've done

00:11:14.019 --> 00:11:17.255
and never once remembered
specifically saying,

00:11:17.255 --> 00:11:18.382
"You got to do that."

00:11:18.382 --> 00:11:22.587
So, it was in there, somehow
it was going after me

00:11:22.587 --> 00:11:25.224
getting them but I never
remember trying to go get 'em.

00:11:25.224 --> 00:11:27.135
Starting with Lincoln
Lawyer was a time I said

00:11:27.135 --> 00:11:29.972
I wanted to recalibrate my
relationship with my career.

00:11:29.972 --> 00:11:31.130
I remember saying that.

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:33.909
I remember thinking my
career, I'm enjoying it.

00:11:33.909 --> 00:11:36.400
I love what it's given
me, I like going to work.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:38.748
I like the things I've
been doing but everything

00:11:38.748 --> 00:11:41.834
I started reading I felt
like, "Oh, it's like it's

00:11:41.834 --> 00:11:43.434
"another version of something I've done

00:11:43.434 --> 00:11:44.943
"or I feel like I could do it next week."

00:11:44.943 --> 00:11:46.229
And, I remember going,
"Well, wait a minute

00:11:46.229 --> 00:11:48.077
"before you get mad at
thinking you could do something

00:11:48.077 --> 00:11:50.480
"next week, that's a valid feeling.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:51.846
"Don't just say no to
something 'cause you feel like

00:11:51.846 --> 00:11:53.371
"you could naturally do it."

00:11:53.371 --> 00:11:56.057
But then I said, "Okay, fair,
I'll shake with you on that,

00:11:56.057 --> 00:11:59.411
"McConaughey, but
(laughs)

00:11:59.411 --> 00:12:02.620
"let me find something
that I don't know how,

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:04.839
"what to do next week or the week after.

00:12:04.839 --> 00:12:06.557
"Let me find something that I really like,

00:12:06.557 --> 00:12:09.540
"but I'm going (vocalizes) I'm not sure.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.133
"Them that scares me,
that shakes my floor."

00:12:12.133 --> 00:12:14.466
And, I was fortunate enough,

00:12:17.062 --> 00:12:19.731
'cause that schedule f check I got

00:12:19.731 --> 00:12:22.178
in Angels in the Outfield did
know that my rent was paid.

00:12:22.178 --> 00:12:23.734
I was fortunate to know
my kids were going to eat.

00:12:23.734 --> 00:12:25.947
I was just shepherding my
first son into the world.

00:12:25.947 --> 00:12:28.637
So, I had that to take care of my time.

00:12:28.637 --> 00:12:30.423
And, I said, "It's a great
time to go into the shadows

00:12:30.423 --> 00:12:34.242
"and be a father and sit
back and see if something

00:12:34.242 --> 00:12:36.854
"turns you on from the inside
or if something finds you

00:12:36.854 --> 00:12:38.458
"if you're the target
that can draw you there.

00:12:38.458 --> 00:12:41.477
"But, we're okay, rent's
paid, kids are going to eat.

00:12:41.477 --> 00:12:43.091
"We can take some time off."

00:12:43.091 --> 00:12:45.390
I in no way was thinking
about, "Like oh, I'm done."

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:46.481
But, I didn't know how long the dry spell

00:12:46.481 --> 00:12:47.314
was going to go.

00:12:47.314 --> 00:12:49.241
So, I said no to some
of the things that were

00:12:49.241 --> 00:12:51.147
similar to what I had been doing before.

00:12:51.147 --> 00:12:53.362
And, it took a while, I had
to sit down with my agent

00:12:53.362 --> 00:12:55.416
and say, "Hey I'm really
going to do this and I need

00:12:55.416 --> 00:12:58.847
"your help with my
commitment to this because

00:12:58.847 --> 00:13:01.448
"after eight months if
the same thing comes in

00:13:01.448 --> 00:13:02.863
"with the big 10 million dollar pay check

00:13:02.863 --> 00:13:05.059
"it starts to read better and funnier."

00:13:05.059 --> 00:13:07.142
(laughs)

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:12.005
I promise you, brother, I
mean they're the same words.

00:13:12.005 --> 00:13:14.601
(laughs)

00:13:14.601 --> 00:13:17.528
All of sudden, I think this thing's great.

00:13:17.528 --> 00:13:20.102
(laughs)

00:13:20.102 --> 00:13:21.727
I think I can find an angle.

00:13:21.727 --> 00:13:24.643
I can make it a different
experience for me.

00:13:24.643 --> 00:13:26.289
So, I said I'm going to need
your help bunkering down here

00:13:26.289 --> 00:13:29.145
and saying no to some things.

00:13:29.145 --> 00:13:30.745
And so, I'm going to need
you to be a gatekeeper

00:13:30.745 --> 00:13:32.347
one to keep from getting too many, too.

00:13:32.347 --> 00:13:35.354
Let's be real clear, I'm looking for some

00:13:35.354 --> 00:13:37.156
different things to do.

00:13:37.156 --> 00:13:39.365
He holed up, he handled that.

00:13:39.365 --> 00:13:42.244
My wife, we said we don't know
how long it's going to be dry.

00:13:42.244 --> 00:13:45.103
Then, after they quit sending
those then it did get dry.

00:13:45.103 --> 00:13:46.829
Then nothing came for about a year,

00:13:46.829 --> 00:13:48.579
- Wow.
- just nothing.

00:13:50.315 --> 00:13:52.634
I didn't have any sleepless
nights or oh my gus.

00:13:52.634 --> 00:13:55.352
It was like, "No, I'll find
something, I'll write."

00:13:55.352 --> 00:13:56.550
I was writing a lot.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:58.707
I was turning myself on
creatively and as I said,

00:13:58.707 --> 00:14:00.891
I had my first son to
shepard into the world.

00:14:00.891 --> 00:14:02.896
And then, a funny thing
happened, a beautiful thing

00:14:02.896 --> 00:14:07.084
happened that somehow the
target drew the arrow.

00:14:07.084 --> 00:14:11.251
And, I think, my opinion
is I became a new good idea

00:14:12.476 --> 00:14:14.059
by removing myself.

00:14:15.442 --> 00:14:17.793
I became William Friedkin
calls for the first time.

00:14:17.793 --> 00:14:19.335
Stephen Soderbergh calls
for the first time.

00:14:19.335 --> 00:14:22.538
Somehow in that two years
I became a fresh, new idea

00:14:22.538 --> 00:14:26.389
that I don't think I was
two to three years prior.

00:14:26.389 --> 00:14:28.259
How that works I'm not
sure because I didn't

00:14:28.259 --> 00:14:29.353
send a memo out.

00:14:29.353 --> 00:14:31.436
(laughs)

00:14:32.471 --> 00:14:33.490
But, it did happen.

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:36.009
Being an actor or even being an artist,

00:14:36.009 --> 00:14:38.487
and I had studied at
school behind the camera

00:14:38.487 --> 00:14:40.953
was not even in the
vernacular of my dreams.

00:14:40.953 --> 00:14:42.482
I was raised nine to five you go to work

00:14:42.482 --> 00:14:44.244
you work your way up a scale.

00:14:44.244 --> 00:14:45.077
- That's from your old man, right?

00:14:45.077 --> 00:14:48.152
- Yeah, and that was
all I was able to even,

00:14:48.152 --> 00:14:50.291
like I said dream about.

00:14:50.291 --> 00:14:53.605
So, the idea of living
life and having a career

00:14:53.605 --> 00:14:55.756
that was in the arts, that was
in the storytelling business,

00:14:55.756 --> 00:14:58.498
that was something that was
really fun was not something

00:14:58.498 --> 00:15:01.581
that, like I said, I even dreamed of.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:05.199
I was in the right place,
right time, right bar.

00:15:05.199 --> 00:15:06.760
A guy found me and said,
"You want to come in and read

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:08.814
"for a part in a movie "I'm here producing

00:15:08.814 --> 00:15:09.773
"called Dazed and Confused."

00:15:09.773 --> 00:15:11.155
I said, "Sure."

00:15:11.155 --> 00:15:13.157
I go read for the part,
it's got three lines.

00:15:13.157 --> 00:15:14.896
Three lines turns into three weeks work

00:15:14.896 --> 00:15:16.702
they pay me $325 bucks a day.

00:15:16.702 --> 00:15:18.195
People are telling me
I'm pretty good at it.

00:15:18.195 --> 00:15:20.413
And, I think I'm getting
away with something.

00:15:20.413 --> 00:15:23.517
And, I still do feel like I'm
getting away with something.

00:15:23.517 --> 00:15:25.057
It's always been the most
important thing for me

00:15:25.057 --> 00:15:28.411
to be the best man that I can be.

00:15:28.411 --> 00:15:32.168
And, my rights as a
human being, number one,

00:15:32.168 --> 00:15:35.711
then my rights as a citizen, number two.

00:15:35.711 --> 00:15:39.544
Then, my rights or my
identity as an American,

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:43.523
a McConaughey, all those things
you can break down to family

00:15:43.523 --> 00:15:47.718
from child of god if that's how you see it

00:15:47.718 --> 00:15:52.472
to being a human to being a
citizen, to being an American

00:15:52.472 --> 00:15:55.142
to being a Mconaughey,
all those identifications

00:15:55.142 --> 00:15:58.377
you get to bringing it down to self.

00:15:58.377 --> 00:16:00.627
Those are all, come before,

00:16:03.457 --> 00:16:05.374
now I'm a famous actor,

00:16:08.571 --> 00:16:11.032
or now I'm an actor who has become famous

00:16:11.032 --> 00:16:12.759
or I am a celebrity.

00:16:12.759 --> 00:16:16.926
Celebrity and fame are amendments
that came along the way.

00:16:20.718 --> 00:16:24.324
Did they all of a sudden
propose many new challenges

00:16:24.324 --> 00:16:25.497
with how to balance life?

00:16:25.497 --> 00:16:27.368
You damn right they did.

00:16:27.368 --> 00:16:29.290
Needed many times, I was
like, "God, can I have

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:32.135
"26 hours in this day instead of 24?

00:16:32.135 --> 00:16:34.430
"I just need two more."

00:16:34.430 --> 00:16:38.142
Man, it's all, and I'm not
sure, I'm not discriminating

00:16:38.142 --> 00:16:40.049
enough, I'm not being discerning enough.

00:16:40.049 --> 00:16:43.093
I'm kind of, everything sounds good.

00:16:43.093 --> 00:16:46.432
Everything, all the
answers were no yesterday

00:16:46.432 --> 00:16:47.767
in this career I'm taking on.

00:16:47.767 --> 00:16:50.281
Boy, over one weekend everything's a yes.

00:16:50.281 --> 00:16:52.200
It invertedly flip flopped.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:54.773
Yesterday I had 99 noes
out of 100 scripts.

00:16:54.773 --> 00:16:56.908
And, over one weekend after
a Time to Kill comes out

00:16:56.908 --> 00:16:58.491
it flips, 99 yeses.

00:16:59.549 --> 00:17:00.948
Great, but hang on a minute.

00:17:00.948 --> 00:17:03.537
I got to, these things that I'm looking at

00:17:03.537 --> 00:17:05.097
I'm going, well yes.

00:17:05.097 --> 00:17:06.577
Three days ago I would
have said yes, to these.

00:17:06.577 --> 00:17:08.623
I have to be much more discriminate

00:17:08.623 --> 00:17:10.823
and really try to go, "let
me ask myself what it is

00:17:10.823 --> 00:17:12.106
"I want to do."

00:17:12.106 --> 00:17:14.665
That's not easy to do
because there's time.

00:17:14.665 --> 00:17:15.731
This was it, more important, "Can I have

00:17:15.731 --> 00:17:17.121
"26 hours in the day?"

00:17:17.121 --> 00:17:19.952
You know what I mean, but I don't have it.

00:17:19.952 --> 00:17:22.119
Disseminating between 'em,

00:17:24.531 --> 00:17:27.476
walking that line of
really respecting this job

00:17:27.476 --> 00:17:29.893
that I do as a real privilege

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:34.300
and an honor to do, at the same time

00:17:38.032 --> 00:17:40.850
you got to go from being impressed

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:43.407
to getting really involved.

00:17:43.407 --> 00:17:45.837
And, I find that if I
can get really involved

00:17:45.837 --> 00:17:50.008
that doesn't lessen my
respect for a craft,

00:17:50.008 --> 00:17:52.149
for something I'm doing.

00:17:52.149 --> 00:17:53.497
It actually raises it
because it's a little more

00:17:53.497 --> 00:17:54.593
of a shoulder look at it.

00:17:54.593 --> 00:17:55.862
It's looking it eye to eye.

00:17:55.862 --> 00:17:57.612
I mean, if you revere

00:17:59.198 --> 00:18:03.097
you're never going to
engage with it as much

00:18:03.097 --> 00:18:05.358
'cause you're always beneath it.

00:18:05.358 --> 00:18:07.691
So, to get involved with it,

00:18:10.114 --> 00:18:13.456
still respect it, don't
patronize it, okay understand it,

00:18:13.456 --> 00:18:15.685
look down on it that's not healthy either.

00:18:15.685 --> 00:18:19.184
But also, while you can respect
it, know it's a privilege

00:18:19.184 --> 00:18:23.604
don't be beneath it, like, "Oh
my god, I'm just so thankful

00:18:23.604 --> 00:18:25.643
"that this is just, oh just do it."

00:18:25.643 --> 00:18:27.193
No, you need to look at it level and go,

00:18:27.193 --> 00:18:29.177
"Okay, but why am I doing
this and what can I give

00:18:29.177 --> 00:18:31.019
"and what can it give me back?"

00:18:31.019 --> 00:18:32.823
There came a time while
we were working there

00:18:32.823 --> 00:18:34.462
I was like, "Okay, the Dallas
Buyer's Club, we got to

00:18:34.462 --> 00:18:37.703
"do that, like it fits in this year.

00:18:37.703 --> 00:18:40.812
"And, I'm going to put it in
that slot, and it was like

00:18:40.812 --> 00:18:42.660
in the fall."

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:46.014
And be like, okay, but it's not real.

00:18:46.014 --> 00:18:46.847
We don't have the financing for it.

00:18:46.847 --> 00:18:49.334
No, no, no we'll get that,
we'll get that, we'll get that.

00:18:49.334 --> 00:18:51.253
It's happening in the fall, in the fall.

00:18:51.253 --> 00:18:54.147
And, part of getting
that and he did Jean-Marc

00:18:54.147 --> 00:18:55.911
he and I'd meet, he's interested in

00:18:55.911 --> 00:18:57.434
some pieces coming together.

00:18:57.434 --> 00:19:00.376
And, what we did is we
didn't flinch once we said

00:19:00.376 --> 00:19:02.002
we were going to make it in that fall.

00:19:02.002 --> 00:19:05.515
Whenever we didn't flinch
when people would go,

00:19:05.515 --> 00:19:08.816
"You can't do this, you
don't have the money for it."

00:19:08.816 --> 00:19:09.707
Which we didn't.

00:19:09.707 --> 00:19:12.175
We're like, "no, no we'll
get it, it's coming.

00:19:12.175 --> 00:19:15.678
"We got it, we're making it in fall."

00:19:15.678 --> 00:19:16.868
And, we just never flinched.

00:19:16.868 --> 00:19:20.114
And I remember I never had any doubt,

00:19:20.114 --> 00:19:21.848
like I talked myself into it obviously

00:19:21.848 --> 00:19:24.002
'cause hell I was wrong,
there wasn't money.

00:19:24.002 --> 00:19:26.120
It caught up with us right
as we were making it,

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:27.482
it caught up with us.

00:19:27.482 --> 00:19:28.416
The money fell out.

00:19:28.416 --> 00:19:31.938
What money was there,
the money had fallen out

00:19:31.938 --> 00:19:33.161
of this thing so many times.

00:19:33.161 --> 00:19:34.650
What money was there and told to be real

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:37.406
turned out to not be real just a few weeks

00:19:37.406 --> 00:19:39.643
before we started shooting.

00:19:39.643 --> 00:19:43.204
And then, by that time
Jean-Marc and I'd be working

00:19:43.204 --> 00:19:46.056
on the script and I had already,

00:19:46.056 --> 00:19:47.964
I was already into this guy and I'd lost

00:19:47.964 --> 00:19:49.488
a bunch of weight someone
said, "We may have to

00:19:49.488 --> 00:19:50.321
"push to Spring."

00:19:50.321 --> 00:19:51.353
And again, I didn't listen, I was like,

00:19:51.353 --> 00:19:53.559
"No, we're not pushing to Spring.

00:19:53.559 --> 00:19:55.235
"We're doing it this fall, we're going.

00:19:55.235 --> 00:19:56.484
"it's set the date, we're there."

00:19:56.484 --> 00:19:59.682
And, something about that
and I wasn't the only one

00:19:59.682 --> 00:20:02.269
doing that, there was others, too.

00:20:02.269 --> 00:20:06.327
The producers and Jean-Marc,
something about that

00:20:06.327 --> 00:20:08.789
it's weird how some things
can get made in this town

00:20:08.789 --> 00:20:10.738
by just blinding saying,
"No, it's happening."

00:20:10.738 --> 00:20:13.267
And, if you get enough
momentum and don't even

00:20:13.267 --> 00:20:15.156
consider the other options.

00:20:15.156 --> 00:20:16.744
'Cause if you flinch,
if we'd have flinched

00:20:16.744 --> 00:20:21.002
and said, "Spring," even that
would have been too much.

00:20:21.002 --> 00:20:23.739
If would have left a sliver of light open

00:20:23.739 --> 00:20:26.102
for somebody to go, "oh,
okay at least it's possible."

00:20:26.102 --> 00:20:29.314
Then, the things that
happened just probably

00:20:29.314 --> 00:20:31.108
wouldn't have happened.

00:20:31.108 --> 00:20:34.878
So, yeah I said yesterday
it was turned down 86 times.

00:20:34.878 --> 00:20:36.889
I got corrected last night, it turned out

00:20:36.889 --> 00:20:40.126
the real count was it's
been turned down 137 times.

00:20:40.126 --> 00:20:41.872
- Wow.
- Wow.

00:20:41.872 --> 00:20:43.455
- So, that is like,

00:20:47.306 --> 00:20:50.306
I don't know what makes this so fun.

00:20:52.006 --> 00:20:54.172
We were never after the result with that,

00:20:54.172 --> 00:20:58.255
just the getting of it
made was so visceral that,

00:20:59.541 --> 00:21:00.928
but now it's around.

00:21:00.928 --> 00:21:05.006
It's a year later and the movie translates

00:21:05.006 --> 00:21:06.708
and then we're getting people
talking about the film,

00:21:06.708 --> 00:21:08.761
the message, our roles.

00:21:08.761 --> 00:21:11.520
It makes you very proud to
be here talking about it

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:13.645
because there was so much
skin left in the game

00:21:13.645 --> 00:21:16.827
by so many people to get it made.

00:21:16.827 --> 00:21:17.968
- Did Jim Morrison provide inspiration

00:21:17.968 --> 00:21:19.426
for the Dazed and Confused character?

00:21:19.426 --> 00:21:20.862
Is that true?

00:21:20.862 --> 00:21:22.809
- Getting ready for that
role the first night

00:21:22.809 --> 00:21:25.300
I went up for a dress
rehearsal and hair and make-up

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:28.279
and I stepped out of the
trailer and Rick Linkletter,

00:21:28.279 --> 00:21:30.325
the director comes up
and goes, "This is great.

00:21:30.325 --> 00:21:31.744
"Look at our Wooderson, this is great."

00:21:31.744 --> 00:21:34.023
And he goes, "Listen
man, I know you're not

00:21:34.023 --> 00:21:35.340
"supposed to work tonight
but we got a scene

00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:38.423
"at the Top Notch, it's Friday night.

00:21:40.042 --> 00:21:42.192
"I think Wooderson might be
there, trolling for chicks

00:21:42.192 --> 00:21:44.205
"what have you, you know
what I mean?" He goes.

00:21:44.205 --> 00:21:45.415
I go, "yeah, he would be there."

00:21:45.415 --> 00:21:47.642
He goes, "What do you
think would Wooderson maybe

00:21:47.642 --> 00:21:49.885
"be attracted to the
redheaded intellectual?

00:21:49.885 --> 00:21:51.155
"'Cause Wooderson's been around school,

00:21:51.155 --> 00:21:53.308
"he's kind of been with
all the typical beauties,

00:21:53.308 --> 00:21:54.363
"what about the redheaded intellectual?

00:21:54.363 --> 00:21:55.196
"Do you think he'd be into that?"

